{"documentId":"tos.work.sextus-empiricus.outlines-of-pyrrhonism","versionId":"retained-outlines-en-forage2026-ai","revision":"5796826c1e9b123b1253409ad3056e735c353f4f4bf94b1bdf84be7251587ff7","units":[{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-60d0faa5cf2b418d9bb2540f7f184421","ordinal":761,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.261","text":"\n\nIf a single apprehension makes the non-expert an expert, then, first, it will be possible to say that an art is not a system of apprehensions; for someone who knew nothing at all would, if taught one theorem of an art, be called an expert on that basis. Then too, suppose someone says that a person who has acquired some theorems of an art and needs one more, and is for that reason a non-expert, is made an expert from a non-expert by a single apprehension if he acquires that one: he will be speaking arbitrarily."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-be87d6d0e6964e948497e3146ce40e19","ordinal":762,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.262","text":"\n\nFor in particular cases he could not point to someone who is still a non-expert but will be an expert if he acquires some one theorem. Surely no one knows the count of the theorems of each art, so as to be able to tally the theorems already known and say how many are left to make up the full number of the art’s theorems. So knowledge of one theorem does not make the non-expert an expert."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-6b0bf2c58dc9444fad0044fc6bf96959","ordinal":763,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.263","text":"\n\nAnd if this is true, then, since no one acquires all the theorems of the arts at once, but, if at all, one at a time (to grant this too for the sake of hypothesis), the person said to acquire the theorems of the art one at a time would not become an expert; for we pointed out that knowledge of a single theorem cannot make a non-expert an expert. So no one becomes an expert from being a non-expert either. Therefore, for these reasons too, the expert appears not to exist; and for this reason neither does the teacher."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-ede472ab965943868b8837b0c82fe4c2","ordinal":764,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.264","text":"\n\nNor can the person said to be learning, being a non-expert, learn and apprehend the theorems of the art in which he is a non-expert. Someone blind from birth, insofar as he is blind, could not get a perception of colors, nor likewise someone deaf from birth a perception of sound; just so, the non-expert could not apprehend the theorems of the art in which he is a non-expert. For in that case the same person would be both an expert and a non-expert in the same things: a non-expert, since that is the hypothesis, and an expert, since he has apprehension of the art’s theorems."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-c480af59790f402e80f5443793f640e9","ordinal":765,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.265","text":"\n\nSo the expert does not teach the non-expert either. And if neither the expert teaches the expert, nor the non-expert the non-expert, nor the non-expert the expert, nor the expert the non-expert, and apart from these there is nothing, then there is neither a teacher nor a person taught.\nSince there is neither learner nor teacher, the method of teaching is superfluous as well."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-882c7926e50842a7bfa5d9ec8b0aff48","ordinal":766,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.266","text":"\n\nBut ⟨it⟩ is no less puzzling for the following reasons. The method of teaching works either by clarity or by speech; but it works neither by clarity nor by speech, as we shall show; so the method of learning is not easy to come by either. Teaching does not work by clarity, since clarity belongs to things that are shown. What is shown is apparent to everyone; what is apparent, insofar as it appears, can be grasped by everyone; and what can be grasped by everyone in common cannot be taught. So nothing is teachable by clarity. Nor, indeed, is anything taught by speech."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-c0ad1eb5597347b6b345fa7eff0215ec","ordinal":767,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.267","text":"\n\nFor speech either signifies something or signifies nothing. If it signifies nothing, it will not teach anything either. If it signifies something, it signifies either by nature or by convention. It does not signify by nature, because not everyone understands everyone when they hear them—Greeks do not understand non-Greeks, for instance, nor non-Greeks Greeks."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-4f987b5f9e7247208ba252e4839f09d3","ordinal":768,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.268","text":"\n\nIf it signifies by convention, it is clear that those who have already apprehended the things the words are assigned to will grasp them—not by being taught by the words what they did not know, but by recalling and renewing what they knew—whereas those who need to learn what they do not know, and do not know what the words are assigned to, will grasp nothing."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-8908e77ba4cf475b8723af743147ead3","ordinal":769,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.269","text":"\n\nSo the method of learning could not exist either. Moreover, the teacher must produce in the learner an apprehension of the theorems of the art being taught, so that the learner, by apprehending the system of them, may thus become an expert. But apprehension is nothing, as we pointed out earlier; so the method of teaching cannot exist either. And if neither the thing taught exists, nor the teacher and the learner, nor the method of learning, then there is neither learning nor teaching."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-cd291897dda44170960de7bc9e1d3e94","ordinal":770,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.270","text":"\n\nThese arguments have been made in fairly general terms about learning and teaching; but one can raise the same puzzles about the so-called art of living in particular. For instance, we showed earlier that the thing taught, namely practical wisdom, does not exist; and the teacher and the learner do not exist either. For either the wise man will teach the art of living to the wise man, or the fool to the fool, or the fool to the wise man, or the wise man to the fool; but none of these teaches any of the others. So the so-called art of living is not taught."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-fb27658791184c0fa41cd6562faa8cc7","ordinal":771,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.271","text":"\n\nAbout the other cases it is perhaps superfluous even to speak. But suppose the wise man teaches the fool practical wisdom. Practical wisdom is knowledge of things good, bad and neither; so the fool, not having practical wisdom, is ignorant of things good, bad and neither. And being ignorant of these, when the wise man teaches him the good, the bad and the neither, he will certainly only hear what is said; he will not know these things. For if he grasped them while remaining in a state of folly, folly too would be capable of discerning things good, bad and neither."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-030e2dcf40d3473bb1109925dbe114cd","ordinal":772,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.272","text":"\n\nBut according to them folly is not capable of discerning these things, since then the fool would be wise. So the fool does not grasp what the wise man says or does in accordance with the principle of practical wisdom. And not grasping it, he could not be taught by him—especially since, as we said before, he cannot be taught either by clarity or through speech. So, then: if the so-called art of living arises in no one, either through learning and teaching or by nature, the art of living that the philosophers keep harping on cannot be found."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-85a3c455d79f4edc825534594153e722","ordinal":773,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.273","text":"\n\nYet even if one were to grant, most generously, that this dreamed-of art of living does arise in someone, it will appear harmful and a cause of turmoil to those who have it, rather than beneficial.\nFor instance, to mention a few examples out of many, the art of living might seem to benefit the wise man by giving him self-control in his impulses toward the fine and his repulsions from the bad."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-08484d5dbdf84577b228ccebbede3331","ordinal":774,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.274","text":"\n\nNow the wise man whom they call self-controlled is called self-controlled either in that he never has any impulse toward the bad or repulsion from the good, or in that he did have bad impulses and repulsions but mastered them by reason."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-0f4ef611d87a491dbbf6688d05af8647","ordinal":775,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.275","text":"\n\nBut in virtue of not having bad judgments he would not be self-controlled; for he will not control what he does not have. No one would call a eunuch self-controlled with regard to sex, or a man with a bad stomach self-controlled with regard to enjoying food (for they have no desire for such things at all, which they might rise above with self-control). In the same way, the wise man should not be called self-controlled either, since no feeling arises in him over which he could exercise control."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-b7d4a382e0a9444e94b67b273ab46a7b","ordinal":776,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.276","text":"\n\nBut if they claim that he is self-controlled in that he does have bad judgments but overcomes them by reason, then, first, they will be granting that practical wisdom has done him no good, since he is still in turmoil and in need of help; and second, he turns out to be more wretched than the people they call bad. For if he has an impulse toward something, he is certainly troubled; and if he masters it by reason, he holds the bad thing inside himself, and for this reason he is more troubled than the bad man, who no longer suffers this."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-f40da8d9cf1043e3990ffb5031687afd","ordinal":777,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.277","text":"\n\nFor the bad man, if he has an impulse, is troubled; but if he gets what he desires, he is rid of his trouble. So the wise man does not become self-controlled, as far as practical wisdom goes; or if he does, he is the most wretched of all human beings, so that the art of living has given him not benefit but the greatest turmoil. And we pointed out earlier that the person who thinks he has the art of living, and through it has recognized what things are good in their nature and what bad, is intensely troubled both when the good things are present to him and when the bad ones are."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-226db32dea164adb805ce0733b5c0a20","ordinal":778,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.278","text":"\n\nWe must say, then, that if the reality of good, bad and indifferent things is not agreed, and the art of living perhaps does not even exist, and, even if it were granted by hypothesis to exist, brings no benefit to those who have it but on the contrary produces the greatest turmoil in them, then the dogmatists would seem to put on their lofty airs for nothing in the so-called ethical part of what they call philosophy as well."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-ebbbd9cdf38a494a894a7172b24156a8","ordinal":779,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.279","text":"\n\nHaving gone through this much about the ethical part too, in due measure, as befits an outline, we here bring to a close both the third book and our whole work, the Outlines of Pyrrhonism, adding just this:"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-0886b7eaf46b41ad9dca46f0b4e489a6","ordinal":780,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.280","text":"\n\nBecause he loves humankind, the skeptic wants to cure the conceit and rashness of the dogmatists by argument, as far as he can. Doctors of bodily ailments have remedies of different strength: to those who are severely affected they apply the strong ones, to those mildly affected the milder ones. In the same way the skeptic propounds arguments that differ [and] in strength:"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-973931db23384baab847892a9a38dd80","ordinal":781,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 3 · §3.281","text":"\n\nagainst those who are badly afflicted with severe rashness he uses weighty arguments, capable of vigorously demolishing the dogmatists’ affliction of conceit; and against those whose affliction of conceit is superficial and easily cured, and can be demolished by milder persuasive arguments, he uses milder ones. That is why someone whose starting point is skepticism does not hesitate to propound arguments that are sometimes weighty in their persuasiveness and sometimes apparently rather feeble. He does so deliberately, since these are often enough for him to achieve his purpose."}]}